#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156993 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157392 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38455e74-50c2-5581-b9e1-a8dd0c85f826" :knowledge-point/title "The Expected Value of the Sample Mean"}] :question/id #uuid "33bedb30-2c99-52e8-a665-e3bddeed7e13" :question/math-academy-id "e-10114" :question/problem "A sample $X_1, X_2$ of two metallic rings is drawn from a population. The population has rings of two different diameters, $16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}$ and $\\textrm{18}\\,\\textrm{mm},$ distributed in the ratio $5:1.$ Calculate $\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}].$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu.$ Then, the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} \n= \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overline{X}$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\mu,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the rings in the population have diameters $16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}$ and $\\textrm{18}\\,\\textrm{mm},$ and these are distributed in the ratio $5:1.$ Therefore, to compute the population mean, we can consider a population consisting of $5+1 = 6$ rings only with the following diameters:\n\n$$\n16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}, \\qquad 16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}, \\qquad 16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}, \\qquad 16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}, \\qquad 16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}, \\qquad 18\\,\\textrm{mm}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(5\\cdot 16.5+18) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(82.5+18) \\\\  & =\\frac{100.5}{6} \\\\  & =16.75\\,\\text{mm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu = 16.75\\,\\textrm{mm}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157172 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157355 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b60ce582-4127-57b8-bba8-04cf17169ca3" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Population Mean From the Sample Mean"}] :question/id #uuid "a485378b-0bce-5576-bcbc-2ef77df2da17" :question/math-academy-id "e-7882" :question/problem "Suppose the following data is sampled from a population. Compute an estimate for the population mean.\n\n$$\n6, \\quad 13, \\quad 7, \\quad 5, \\quad 14 , \\quad 11, \\quad 7\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with the population mean $\\mu.$ \nThen, the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} \n= \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i.\n$$\n\nNote that:\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the mean value of $X_1,X_2,\\ldots, X_n,$ while $\\bar{x}$ is the mean of a specific sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n,$\n\n- the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is an estimator of the population mean $\\mu,$ and\n\n- the value $\\hat{\\mu} = \\bar{x}$ is an estimate of the population mean $\\mu.$\n\nTherefore, our estimate for the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\mu } & =\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}(6+13+7+5+14+11+7) \\\\  & =\\frac{63}{7} \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433282 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157355 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b60ce582-4127-57b8-bba8-04cf17169ca3" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Population Mean From the Sample Mean"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a29066f-c5c6-5f13-9704-332d81aafa1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433223} {:answer/id #uuid "1e63e961-b7cd-57a7-b177-22138ad08e0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433227} {:answer/id #uuid "2c9c2705-b383-5552-8765-afa77e795806" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433230} {:answer/id #uuid "9d1a5509-8a63-5798-9db0-2a0cde6f6ed1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433251} {:answer/id #uuid "ef41a907-369a-5ca3-9f40-ac473c2bc7f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef41a907-369a-5ca3-9f40-ac473c2bc7f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433262} :answer-field/id #uuid "89547a27-782d-5edb-a84a-c342d94aab57" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433268}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fac2f103-d2fc-584f-ab2c-f1bc5b2aaa60" :question/math-academy-id "q-139803" :question/problem "Suppose the following data is sampled from a population. Compute an estimate for the population mean.\n\n$$\n-9,\\,-4,\\,1,\\,7,\\,-3,\\,6,\\,10,\\,12\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with the population mean $\\mu .$ \nThen, the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}.\n$$\n\nNote that:\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the mean value of ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n},$ while $\\overset{¯}{x}$ is the mean of a specific sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$\n\n- the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is an estimator of the population mean $\\mu ,$ and\n\n- the value $\\overset{^}{\\mu }=\\overset{¯}{x}$ is an estimate of the population mean $\\mu .$\n\nTherefore, our estimate for the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\mu } & =\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(-9-4+1+7-3+6+10+12) \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{8} \\\\  & =2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157599 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157392 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38455e74-50c2-5581-b9e1-a8dd0c85f826" :knowledge-point/title "The Expected Value of the Sample Mean"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2936c8de-b762-59f1-bba9-735fbc48a942" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.25\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412706} {:answer/id #uuid "46d59859-24c5-5ddd-ac89-50051b7c27c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412710} {:answer/id #uuid "4c983c9b-b809-585e-97ec-8977702a501c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412711} {:answer/id #uuid "607fa761-c4b1-5186-83ab-55c0ba40778f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.5\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412714} {:answer/id #uuid "f00c7066-a8f5-5648-b370-55d63c9722ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.5\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412740}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "607fa761-c4b1-5186-83ab-55c0ba40778f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.5\\,\\text{cm}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412714} :answer-field/id #uuid "5decded0-8969-541f-936f-5b210c73460d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1ea5fc2c-4d2b-5104-893c-7896d0999b42" :question/math-academy-id "q-143190" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2}$ of two nails is drawn from a population. The population has nails of two different lengths, $6\\,\\text{cm}$ and $\\text{12}\\,\\text{cm},$ distributed in the ratio $3:1.$ Calculate $\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}].$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu .$ Then, the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{―}{X}$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\mu ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu .\n$$\n\nWe're told that the nails in the population have lengths $6\\,\\text{cm}$ and $\\text{12}\\,\\text{cm},$ and these are distributed in the ratio $3:1.$ Therefore, to compute the population mean, we can consider a population consisting of $3+1=4$ nails only with the following lengths:\n\n$$\n6\\,\\text{cm},\\,6\\,\\text{cm},\\,6\\,\\text{cm},\\,12\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(3\\cdot 6+12) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(18+12) \\\\  & =\\frac{30}{4} \\\\  & =7.5\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu =7.5\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433280 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157392 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38455e74-50c2-5581-b9e1-a8dd0c85f826" :knowledge-point/title "The Expected Value of the Sample Mean"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00d00815-bcdc-5d4c-8d9c-9110e655684d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15.5}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433220} {:answer/id #uuid "2939ee74-3320-5827-9605-fa3dc1556af8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 175921